WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.632
Brown: Joe and I met in 1966

00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:10.666
when we began our

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:12.232
internships at the Massachussetts

00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:13.232
General Hospital

00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:14.166
in Internal Medicine.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:16.366
I had been a

00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:17.299
medical student at the

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.566
University of Pennsylvania,

00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:19.132
the nation's

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:20.999
oldest medical school,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.299
and Joe had been a...

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.632
had graduated from

00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:25.432
Southwestern Medical College

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:26.232
in Dallas, Texas,

00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:27.766
the nation's youngest

00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:28.732
medical school

00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:30.732
that I had never heard of.

00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:31.599
I thought it was a

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.966
Bible school.

00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:36.332
But, within two days after

00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:38.332
we began our internship,

00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:40.199
we realized that we had

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.832
something in common.

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:42.432
We were both interested

00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:43.466
in the fundamental

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:44.132
basis of disease.

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:45.766
Not only in taking

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:46.899
care of our patients,

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:47.266
which was

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.066
our first responsibility,

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:49.932
but also

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:50.632
trying to figure out

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:52.532
why this person

00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:53.132
had a heart attack?

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.166
Why this person

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:54.932
had rheumatoid arthritis?

00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:56.232
Why did this person

00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:57.399
have pneumonia?

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.532
And it was that interest

00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:01.766
in the basis of disease

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:03.566
that created

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:04.832
this friendship that

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:07.766
has now lasted since 1966.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.866
After our residencies

00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:10.799
we went to the

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.232
National Institutes of Health,

00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:13.966
where both of

00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:16.132
us worked with very

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:17.232
exceptional scientists.

00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:18.499
Joe with Marshall Nirenberg

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.766
and I with Earl Stadtman,

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:21.466
two of the best,

00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:23.032
most brilliant

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:24.832
scientists at NIH,

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.399
but we also had clinical duties,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:27.832
and there we

00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:29.666
saw a patient

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:30.432
that actually determined

00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:31.999
the scientific course

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.766
of the rest of our lives.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:36.799
Goldstein: So the patient

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:37.899
that Mike and I saw

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.432
at the NIH was a little girl

00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:43.266
the first patient,

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.066
with a disease called

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:46.799
familial hypercholesterolemia.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.732
Also called

00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:49.299
homozygous familial

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.166
hypercholesterolemia

00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:53.499
or FH. And her brother

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.232
actually had

00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:55.432
the same disorder,

00:01:55.433 --> 00:01:57.366
and this little girl

00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:59.766
has a very high cholesterol.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:01.366
Her cholesterol is as high

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:03.732
as 800 to 900

00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:07.066
milligrams per deciliter.

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:07.799
The normal of a

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.199
child of this age

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:10.766
would be about a 120

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:12.366
milligrams per deciliter.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:13.532
And most of this

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.732
high cholesterol

00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:16.832
was in a

00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:18.866
lipoprotein called LDL

00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:20.132
which is known

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:21.066
to everyone as the

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:23.332
bad form of cholesterol.

00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:25.132
And when LDL

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:27.432
accumulates in the blood

00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:28.666
as in this little girl

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:31.166
it deposits in the skin

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:31.566
in these little

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:32.766
yellow lumps you see

00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:34.399
on the surface of her skin.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.032
They are called xanthomas.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.632
And it also accumulates

00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:40.099
in the artery wall

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:40.666
and produces

00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:43.599
coronary heart disease,

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.666
and this little girl

00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:47.499
had a heart attack

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:47.966
when she was

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.499
six years of age.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.066
Now, this little girl

00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:56.366
had only elevated LDL.

00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:57.066
She had no high

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:57.299
blood pressure.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.466
She had no diabetes.

00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:01.299
She didn't smoke.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:01.666
She didn't have a

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:02.932
Type A personality.

00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:04.566
Her only risk factor

00:03:04.567 --> 00:03:05.466
for having a heart attack

00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:07.099
at age 6

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.732
was this high level of LDL.

00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:09.499
So this was one of the

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.866
best examples of

00:03:13.867 --> 00:03:14.766
a disease where we

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:16.299
really know the cause.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:16.999
And the cause of the disease

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.432
in this little girl

00:03:18.433 --> 00:03:24.332
is an increased level of her LDL.

00:03:24.333 --> 00:03:28.699
And so it will be

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.232
important to understand

00:03:30.233 --> 00:03:31.166
the basis of why

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:33.399
her LDL is elevated

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:33.799
and also how it

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.432
causes her disease.

00:03:35.433 --> 00:03:38.032
Brown: This is LDL.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:40.566
The lipoprotein that builds up

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:41.466
in the plasma of this

00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:42.032
young girl.

00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:46.132
It's a spherical particle

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:47.599
that's about the size

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.032
of a small virus.

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:50.032
And each LDL particle

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:52.632
has 1500 molecules of

00:03:52.633 --> 00:03:54.566
cholesterol within its core.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:54.999
And it's surrounded by

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.499
a polar coat of phospholipid

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.366
and a protein called ApoB.

00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:01.432
And LDL circulates

00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:02.866
in the blood

00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:03.566
on average for about

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:04.566
three days

00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:05.299
in humans

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.932
and eventually it leaves

00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:07.566
the blood stream.

00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:09.799
When we began our work,

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:10.932
no one knew anything

00:04:10.933 --> 00:04:11.966
about what controlled

00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:13.166
the level of LDL

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:15.566
in the blood.

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:15.766
We were interested

00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:17.766
in this disease because

00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:19.732
not only do the

00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:22.066
homozygotes have problems,

00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:23.232
but the heterozygotes,

00:04:23.233 --> 00:04:24.232
people who inherit

00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:25.799
one copy of the

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.132
abnormal gene

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:27.966
have a moderately

00:04:27.967 --> 00:04:29.466
elevated cholesterol,

00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:30.866
and they have heart attacks

00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:31.832
not at age six,

00:04:31.833 --> 00:04:33.032
but at age sixty.

00:04:33.033 --> 00:04:36.032
And so if you ignore

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:36.899
the homozygotes,

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:37.932
the heterozygotes

00:04:37.933 --> 00:04:39.232
transmit their disease

00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:40.599
in an autosomal

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:41.432
dominant fashion

00:04:41.433 --> 00:04:44.799
through the pedigree.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.799
And so having a single copy

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:48.866
of the mutant gene,

00:04:48.867 --> 00:04:49.799
no matter what it is,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.399
produced the disease.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.499
Now at the time,

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:54.932
there were about 35

00:04:54.933 --> 00:04:56.599
single gene diseases

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.032
that had been worked out

00:04:58.033 --> 00:04:59.799
and the defective gene

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.132
product was known.

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:04.799
All 35 of those were enzymes.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.032
Mutations in enzymes,

00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:07.666
and all of them

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:09.732
were autosomal recessive.

00:05:09.733 --> 00:05:10.099
That means that the

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.099
heterozygote had no symptoms.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.932
But in the case of

00:05:13.933 --> 00:05:15.299
familial hypercholesterolemia

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.132
the heterozygote had a disease.

00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:18.666
And we thought that

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:20.432
if we could understand this disease

00:05:20.433 --> 00:05:22.066
it would be the first

00:05:22.067 --> 00:05:23.232
dominant disease

00:05:23.233 --> 00:05:25.066
that could be elucidated

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:25.632
in humans,

00:05:25.633 --> 00:05:27.232
and it might teach us

00:05:27.233 --> 00:05:28.232
something about

00:05:28.233 --> 00:05:30.832
proteins that are not enzymes.

00:05:30.833 --> 00:05:31.232
We assumed that it

00:05:31.233 --> 00:05:33.199
was not going to be an enzyme.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.166
And that it would probably

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:35.466
be some disorder

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:36.666
of regulation.

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:38.999
Now Joe had promised

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.832
his professor at

00:05:40.833 --> 00:05:41.832
Southwestern Medical

00:05:41.833 --> 00:05:43.366
College that he would

00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:46.766
return there after his training.

00:05:46.767 --> 00:05:49.432
And Joe and I talked about this,

00:05:49.433 --> 00:05:50.899
and Joe, and we decided

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:52.699
that I would also go to Dallas.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:53.799
And we went.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.066
And when we went to Dallas,

00:05:56.067 --> 00:05:56.332
we would both try

00:05:56.333 --> 00:05:59.366
to figure out what type of defect

00:05:59.367 --> 00:06:03.066
could cause this high LDL level.

00:06:03.067 --> 00:06:03.732
And the unique approach

00:06:03.733 --> 00:06:04.999
that we took was

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.799
to study the metabolism

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.599
of cholesterol

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.432
in cultured cells.

00:06:10.433 --> 00:06:11.299
We obtained biopsies

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.332
from children with

00:06:13.333 --> 00:06:14.499
familial hypercholesterolemia

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:15.532
and compared them

00:06:15.533 --> 00:06:16.566
with normal cells

00:06:16.567 --> 00:06:18.066
and studied the

00:06:18.067 --> 00:06:19.366
regulation of cholesterol

00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:21.166
synthesis, because we thought that

00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:21.899
this would be some

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.066
regulatory abnormality.

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:27.666
So here is an experiment

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:29.466
that Mike and I

00:06:29.467 --> 00:06:31.999
carried out in 1973.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.666
And it is arguably,

00:06:33.667 --> 00:06:36.999
probably, the most exciting experiment

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.032
that we've ever done

00:06:39.033 --> 00:06:40.299
and it really hooked us

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.066
on science

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:43.332
and also taught us a lesson

00:06:43.333 --> 00:06:44.499
that I will come back to

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.366
after describing the experiment.

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:48.832
So as Mike prointed out,

00:06:48.833 --> 00:06:49.599
we wanted to understand

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.166
what there was about

00:06:53.167 --> 00:06:54.666
the regulation of cholesterol synthesis

00:06:54.667 --> 00:06:56.366
in these children

00:06:56.367 --> 00:06:58.432
with homozygous FH

00:06:58.433 --> 00:07:00.532
that was different from normal.

00:07:00.533 --> 00:07:01.899
And so, at the time

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:04.732
it was known from studies

00:07:04.733 --> 00:07:05.832
in rats and mice

00:07:05.833 --> 00:07:07.832
that when you fed

00:07:07.833 --> 00:07:11.499
one of these small animals cholesterol

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:12.332
you would suppress

00:07:12.333 --> 00:07:13.732
cholesterol synthesis

00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:14.032
in the liver.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.366
That was a hint that

00:07:15.367 --> 00:07:17.732
there was a regulatory system

00:07:17.733 --> 00:07:18.632
and that the liver

00:07:18.633 --> 00:07:20.666
was known to be the

00:07:20.667 --> 00:07:22.032
most important organ

00:07:22.033 --> 00:07:22.732
in the body for

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:23.766
controlling cholesterol,

00:07:23.767 --> 00:07:24.632
but there was no way

00:07:24.633 --> 00:07:26.466
that we could get liver biopsies

00:07:26.467 --> 00:07:27.432
from patients.

00:07:27.433 --> 00:07:29.866
So we chose fibroblasts

00:07:29.867 --> 00:07:32.432
which one could get,

00:07:32.433 --> 00:07:33.332
skin fibroblasts, and this was

00:07:33.333 --> 00:07:34.866
sort of a heretical

00:07:34.867 --> 00:07:35.999
idea at the time

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.199
that one would study

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.132
a fundamental process

00:07:38.133 --> 00:07:40.166
that was going on in the liver

00:07:40.167 --> 00:07:41.899
in a fibroblast,

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:43.066
but we did it anyway.

00:07:43.067 --> 00:07:46.032
And here's the experiment.

00:07:46.033 --> 00:07:46.666
And the first experiment

00:07:46.667 --> 00:07:47.766
we did worked,

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:50.699
and it was really quite dramatic.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.466
So what we did was to compare

00:07:53.467 --> 00:07:55.166
fibroblasts grown

00:07:55.167 --> 00:07:57.966
in fetal calf serum

00:07:57.967 --> 00:07:59.066
which contains LDL

00:07:59.067 --> 00:08:00.732
from a normal subject

00:08:00.733 --> 00:08:01.799
and one of these

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:02.766
children with homozygous

00:08:02.767 --> 00:08:04.666
familial hypercholesterolemia.

00:08:04.667 --> 00:08:08.132
And you can see in the red curve

00:08:08.133 --> 00:08:12.566
on the left that

00:08:12.567 --> 00:08:17.132
the level of the

00:08:17.133 --> 00:08:18.366
cholesterol synthesis

00:08:18.367 --> 00:08:19.666
was very low,

00:08:19.667 --> 00:08:21.232
almost undetectable.

00:08:21.233 --> 00:08:23.599
Well, now, as a surrogate

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.732
for a cholesterol synthesis,

00:08:25.733 --> 00:08:26.132
we use the enzyme

00:08:26.133 --> 00:08:27.399
HMG CoA reductase

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.066
which had been known from

00:08:29.067 --> 00:08:29.999
these animal studies

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.099
to be a key rate controlling enzyme

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:33.799
in the cholesterol

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:34.932
biosynthetic pathway

00:08:34.933 --> 00:08:35.366
so we were able

00:08:35.367 --> 00:08:37.199
to set up an assay

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.632
for this enzyme

00:08:38.633 --> 00:08:39.732
and we would after incubating

00:08:39.733 --> 00:08:41.932
the cells with this

00:08:41.933 --> 00:08:42.866
fetal calf serum that

00:08:42.867 --> 00:08:43.666
contained LDL,

00:08:43.667 --> 00:08:44.699
we would make extracts

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:45.866
and do the measurements.

00:08:45.867 --> 00:08:47.032
So you can see that

00:08:47.033 --> 00:08:48.599
at zero time,

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.432
in the red curve,

00:08:50.433 --> 00:08:52.166
the level was very low.

00:08:52.167 --> 00:08:53.266
And then when we

00:08:53.267 --> 00:08:56.099
removed  the serum

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:57.766
and the LDL from the media

00:08:57.767 --> 00:08:59.499
for the next 24 hours,

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:00.766
the enzyme activity

00:09:00.767 --> 00:09:02.132
of the normal cells

00:09:02.133 --> 00:09:05.132
in red went up 50-100 fold.

00:09:05.133 --> 00:09:06.899
And then when we

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:07.899
on the right panel

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:10.432
when we removed... added LDL back

00:09:10.433 --> 00:09:11.732
you can see that

00:09:11.733 --> 00:09:13.032
that very high level

00:09:13.033 --> 00:09:14.766
of the enzyme HMG CoA reductase

00:09:14.767 --> 00:09:17.932
came down to

00:09:17.933 --> 00:09:20.899
the basal level when the LDL

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:22.099
was added back.

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:22.832
So this was a...

00:09:22.833 --> 00:09:24.799
this in itself was a

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:26.099
very interesting experiment

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:26.866
in the normal

00:09:26.867 --> 00:09:27.666
because it told us that

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:29.732
there was an effective

00:09:29.733 --> 00:09:31.766
LDL cholesterol

00:09:31.767 --> 00:09:33.266
regulating the activity

00:09:33.267 --> 00:09:34.366
of this enzyme

00:09:34.367 --> 00:09:36.232
that reflected

00:09:36.233 --> 00:09:38.066
the cholesterol biosynthetic pathway

00:09:38.067 --> 00:09:39.099
in the normals.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.399
That in itself was exciting,

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.632
but the real dramatic result

00:09:42.633 --> 00:09:45.266
was when this result

00:09:45.267 --> 00:09:46.132
in the normal

00:09:46.133 --> 00:09:46.399
was compared

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:47.932
to the FH homozygote

00:09:47.933 --> 00:09:49.066
shown in yellow.

00:09:49.067 --> 00:09:50.532
And you can see that at zero time

00:09:50.533 --> 00:09:51.999
in the presence of

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.532
fetal calf serum

00:09:53.533 --> 00:09:54.632
and LDL, the level

00:09:54.633 --> 00:09:55.532
of the enzyme

00:09:55.533 --> 00:09:57.266
HMG CoA reductase

00:09:57.267 --> 00:09:58.566
and cholesterol synthesis

00:09:58.567 --> 00:09:59.399
was a hundred times

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.366
higher than in the normal

00:10:01.367 --> 00:10:03.532
and it didn't change

00:10:03.533 --> 00:10:04.799
no matter what we did.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:06.066
When we removed the LDL

00:10:06.067 --> 00:10:07.266
it stayed high, and when

00:10:07.267 --> 00:10:08.366
we added LDL back

00:10:08.367 --> 00:10:09.499
it stayed high.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:10.066
So this was

00:10:10.067 --> 00:10:11.632
really dramatic.

00:10:11.633 --> 00:10:13.332
It was really all or none

00:10:13.333 --> 00:10:15.299
and that was one of the things

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:16.166
that I think not only

00:10:16.167 --> 00:10:19.932
was it an exciting result, but it

00:10:19.933 --> 00:10:22.066
sort of addicted us to the

00:10:22.067 --> 00:10:26.532
fact that we were really

00:10:26.533 --> 00:10:27.199
interested in

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:28.566
all future experiments

00:10:28.567 --> 00:10:29.566
and seeing big changes.

00:10:29.567 --> 00:10:31.299
We don't usually see

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.032
changes of a hundred fold

00:10:33.033 --> 00:10:34.999
but at least it told us

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:35.766
that one had to have

00:10:35.767 --> 00:10:36.566
a really big change

00:10:36.567 --> 00:10:37.732
to really work out something.

00:10:37.733 --> 00:10:40.999
Now our simplest interpretation

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.199
from this result when Mike and I

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:47.399
sat down and tried to

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:51.299
deal with it after 24 hours of being

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.632
off the wall with excitement.

00:10:54.633 --> 00:10:56.132
We decided that

00:10:56.133 --> 00:10:57.532
the simplest interpretation was

00:10:57.533 --> 00:10:59.966
that the FH homozygote

00:10:59.967 --> 00:11:01.332
cells had a mutation

00:11:01.333 --> 00:11:02.066
in their enzyme

00:11:02.067 --> 00:11:03.466
HMG CoA reductase

00:11:03.467 --> 00:11:05.132
that rendered the enzyme

00:11:05.133 --> 00:11:06.332
resistant to feedback

00:11:06.333 --> 00:11:08.266
regulation by cholesterol.

00:11:08.267 --> 00:11:09.899
So we said, ok, well, if that is true

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.232
then we should be able to

00:11:11.233 --> 00:11:13.466
add cholesterol not in LDL

00:11:13.467 --> 00:11:14.432
but in ethanol

00:11:14.433 --> 00:11:16.699
and we'd get the same result.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.666
But then when we did that

00:11:19.667 --> 00:11:22.632
the curve, the yellow curve,

00:11:22.633 --> 00:11:24.032
the FH homozygote

00:11:24.033 --> 00:11:25.932
came down

00:11:25.933 --> 00:11:27.699
from this hundred fold

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:29.499
just like the normal.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:30.766
And so the normal

00:11:30.767 --> 00:11:31.899
and the FH homozygote

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:32.699
cells were now

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:33.266
responding

00:11:33.267 --> 00:11:34.632
to the cholesterol in ethanol

00:11:34.633 --> 00:11:37.666
whereas the FH homozygote

00:11:37.667 --> 00:11:40.732
did not respond to the LDL.

00:11:40.733 --> 00:11:43.166
So that then told us that

00:11:43.167 --> 00:11:44.399
our original hypothesis

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:45.332
was wrong and now

00:11:45.333 --> 00:11:46.699
we have a new hypothesis

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:48.199
that said that the

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:49.066
defect in the

00:11:49.067 --> 00:11:50.632
FH homozygote was a

00:11:50.633 --> 00:11:51.699
failure to extract

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:53.832
the cholesterol from LDL.

00:11:53.833 --> 00:11:55.032
So how does

00:11:55.033 --> 00:11:55.832
a normal cell...

00:11:55.833 --> 00:11:57.132
This now became the question...

00:11:57.133 --> 00:11:58.299
How does a normal cell

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.232
extract the cholesterol

00:12:00.233 --> 00:12:01.299
from LDL.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:03.799
Brown: So we radiolabeled

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:05.699
LDL with iodine-125

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:06.432
and incubated it

00:12:06.433 --> 00:12:08.499
with the fibroblasts.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:09.932
And what we saw

00:12:09.933 --> 00:12:12.632
was that when we incubated

00:12:12.633 --> 00:12:14.232
the LDL with normal fibroblasts

00:12:14.233 --> 00:12:16.366
there was high affinity

00:12:16.367 --> 00:12:18.166
cell surface binding that was

00:12:18.167 --> 00:12:19.199
saturatable as shown by

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:22.732
the red line on this slide.

00:12:22.733 --> 00:12:23.966
But when we incubated

00:12:23.967 --> 00:12:25.466
the LDL with the

00:12:25.467 --> 00:12:27.132
FH homozygote fibroblasts

00:12:27.133 --> 00:12:28.699
there was no binding.

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:31.999
So the problem was

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.032
that there must have been

00:12:34.033 --> 00:12:35.166
a receptor on the cell

00:12:35.167 --> 00:12:37.499
surface that binds LDL

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.766
and the FH homozygote cells

00:12:39.767 --> 00:12:41.099
lacked this receptor.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:42.766
Now this was 1974.

00:12:42.767 --> 00:12:45.732
It was the very, very early days.

00:12:45.733 --> 00:12:46.832
Receptors were kind of

00:12:46.833 --> 00:12:48.766
theoretical concepts.

00:12:48.767 --> 00:12:49.866
How they worked

00:12:49.867 --> 00:12:51.832
was.... there was

00:12:51.833 --> 00:12:52.466
very little known

00:12:52.467 --> 00:12:54.432
about how they actually worked.

00:12:54.433 --> 00:12:58.932
But we went one step further

00:12:58.933 --> 00:13:00.199
when we incubated

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:02.332
the cells with I-125 LDL.

00:13:02.333 --> 00:13:03.632
We followed what

00:13:03.633 --> 00:13:04.932
happened to the LDL

00:13:04.933 --> 00:13:07.699
after the binding reaction.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:09.399
And we were shocked to find

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:11.632
that the I-125 LDL

00:13:11.633 --> 00:13:13.132
was totally degraded.

00:13:13.133 --> 00:13:14.966
The protein was degraded

00:13:14.967 --> 00:13:17.099
down to amino acids.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:18.332
And  the only way that could happen

00:13:18.333 --> 00:13:21.099
would be if the LDL had been

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:22.466
taken into the cell

00:13:22.467 --> 00:13:23.599
and degraded

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:24.966
in the digestive organelle

00:13:24.967 --> 00:13:25.532
of the cell

00:13:25.533 --> 00:13:26.632
which is the lysosome.

00:13:26.633 --> 00:13:28.499
Goldstein: So once we knew

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:30.432
that there was

00:13:30.433 --> 00:13:31.832
a putative receptor

00:13:31.833 --> 00:13:33.666
on the surface of the cells

00:13:33.667 --> 00:13:35.299
we advanced the concept

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.432
of an LDL receptor

00:13:36.433 --> 00:13:38.999
pathway in which the LDL bound to

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.332
receptors on the cell surface

00:13:41.333 --> 00:13:43.199
in specialized regions called

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:44.699
coated pits.

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:48.432
And our colleague and collaborator

00:13:48.433 --> 00:13:49.432
Richard Anderson was

00:13:49.433 --> 00:13:50.132
able to demonstrate

00:13:50.133 --> 00:13:52.332
by electron microscopy

00:13:52.333 --> 00:13:53.332
the localization

00:13:53.333 --> 00:13:55.866
of ferritin labeled LDL

00:13:55.867 --> 00:13:57.232
in these coated pits.

00:13:57.233 --> 00:13:57.732
Coated pits are only

00:13:57.733 --> 00:13:59.866
about 2% of the cell surface

00:13:59.867 --> 00:14:01.632
of a fibroblast

00:14:01.633 --> 00:14:03.232
and so it's a very

00:14:03.233 --> 00:14:04.399
specialized region

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:05.932
for rapid endocytosis

00:14:05.933 --> 00:14:07.632
and internalization

00:14:07.633 --> 00:14:12.166
of proteins like LDL.

00:14:12.167 --> 00:14:12.832
So once the LDL

00:14:12.833 --> 00:14:14.399
bound the coated pit,

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:14.866
the coated pits

00:14:14.867 --> 00:14:17.099
pinched off to form coated vesicles

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:18.399
which delivered the LDL

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:20.699
to endosomes

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:22.066
and then ultimately to the lysosomes

00:14:22.067 --> 00:14:24.332
where the LDL was degraded

00:14:24.333 --> 00:14:26.832
and the cholesterol was released,

00:14:26.833 --> 00:14:27.832
and that cholesterol

00:14:27.833 --> 00:14:30.232
was then used

00:14:30.233 --> 00:14:30.599
by the cell

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:31.932
for membrane synthesis.

00:14:31.933 --> 00:14:33.366
So we now knew

00:14:33.367 --> 00:14:35.632
from the LDL receptor pathway

00:14:35.633 --> 00:14:38.132
that in addition to

00:14:38.133 --> 00:14:40.499
obtaining cholesterol

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:42.266
from cholesterol synthesis,

00:14:42.267 --> 00:14:43.432
cells could also obtain

00:14:43.433 --> 00:14:45.332
cholesterol through the

00:14:45.333 --> 00:14:47.266
receptor mediated endocytosis

00:14:47.267 --> 00:14:49.299
of LDL, and this cholesterol

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:51.432
not only

00:14:51.433 --> 00:14:53.232
was used for the synthesis

00:14:53.233 --> 00:14:55.366
of plasma membranes, but also

00:14:55.367 --> 00:14:58.366
it exerted the feedback regulation

00:14:58.367 --> 00:14:59.266
on the enzymes

00:14:59.267 --> 00:15:00.432
involved in the cholesterol

00:15:00.433 --> 00:15:01.966
biosynthetic pathway.

00:15:01.967 --> 00:15:05.232
And it also exerted a feedback

00:15:05.233 --> 00:15:07.299
suppression on the gene

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:08.766
for the LDL receptor

00:15:08.767 --> 00:15:10.299
so one had a coordinate

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:13.499
feedback regulatory system

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:17.066
based on both entry from LDL

00:15:17.067 --> 00:15:19.832
and the biosynthesis.

00:15:19.833 --> 00:15:22.432
So what happened

00:15:22.433 --> 00:15:25.199
in the FH homozygote cells

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:26.332
that lacked LDL receptor?

00:15:26.333 --> 00:15:26.832
How did they

00:15:26.833 --> 00:15:28.932
obtain their cholesterol

00:15:28.933 --> 00:15:31.066
for membrane synthesis

00:15:31.067 --> 00:15:32.999
and maintain cholesterol homeostasis?

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.566
So in these cells they

00:15:35.567 --> 00:15:38.366
rely virtually completely on

00:15:38.367 --> 00:15:40.799
the cholesterol biosynthetic pathway

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:46.199
and that is their source of cholesterol.

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:49.066
So the cell is happy

00:15:49.067 --> 00:15:51.966
because the cell has its cholesterol

00:15:51.967 --> 00:15:55.299
for its membrane synthesis,

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:57.399
but it's all at the expense

00:15:57.400 --> 00:15:58.166
of an elevated

00:15:58.167 --> 00:16:01.032
LDL in the bloodstream

00:16:01.033 --> 00:16:01.966
which is the root of

00:16:01.967 --> 00:16:02.499
this disease,

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:04.399
familial hypercholesterolemia

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:05.699
when the LDL builds up

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:07.799
and ultimately deposits in the skin

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:08.832
, in these xanthomas,

00:16:08.833 --> 00:16:10.399
and in the artery wall

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:13.099
and produces atherosclerosis.

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:16.999
Brown: So over the next few years

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:18.566
we were able to define

00:16:18.567 --> 00:16:21.366
in detail the pathway by which

00:16:21.367 --> 00:16:22.266
LDL delivers

00:16:22.267 --> 00:16:23.832
the cholesterols to cells

00:16:23.833 --> 00:16:25.999
and it is shown on this slide.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:28.199
After binding to the receptor in coated pits,

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.232
the LDL is internalized,

00:16:30.233 --> 00:16:32.632
and it is delivered to endosomes,

00:16:32.633 --> 00:16:34.199
and within endosomes

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.366
there are proton pumps

00:16:36.367 --> 00:16:38.232
that acidify the interior

00:16:38.233 --> 00:16:39.332
of the endosome.

00:16:39.333 --> 00:16:41.599
That causes a conformational change

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:42.832
in the receptor

00:16:42.833 --> 00:16:45.699
so that it releases the LDL particle.

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:48.566
The LDL is then carried further

00:16:48.567 --> 00:16:49.832
into the lysosomes,

00:16:49.833 --> 00:16:51.866
where it is totally degraded

00:16:51.867 --> 00:16:53.799
and the cholesterol is liberated

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:56.232
for use to make new membranes.

00:16:56.233 --> 00:16:58.066
LDL derived cholesterol

00:16:58.067 --> 00:17:00.266
is also used for steroid hormones,

00:17:00.267 --> 00:17:01.766
bile acids.

00:17:01.767 --> 00:17:03.166
It is used to make

00:17:03.167 --> 00:17:04.866
new lipoproteins, and

00:17:04.867 --> 00:17:06.266
as Joe mentioned,

00:17:06.267 --> 00:17:07.799
it exerts regulatory actions.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.732
So the LDL derived cholesterol

00:17:09.733 --> 00:17:11.632
is really important biologically.

00:17:11.633 --> 00:17:15.132
After the LDL dissociates

00:17:15.133 --> 00:17:17.032
from the receptor, the receptor

00:17:17.033 --> 00:17:18.366
returns to the cell surface

00:17:18.367 --> 00:17:20.932
in a recycling endosome.

00:17:20.933 --> 00:17:22.766
And each of these,

00:17:22.767 --> 00:17:24.632
each LDL receptor

00:17:24.633 --> 00:17:27.299
makes one trip into and out of

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:29.799
the cell every 20 minutes.

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:30.799
And it makes

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:32.699
hundreds of trips during its

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:33.932
lifetime,  and each

00:17:33.933 --> 00:17:36.032
time it carries an LDL particle

00:17:36.033 --> 00:17:37.332
with 1500 molecules

00:17:37.333 --> 00:17:39.566
of cholesterol ester.

00:17:39.567 --> 00:17:40.932
We gave this process

00:17:40.933 --> 00:17:41.932
its general name

00:17:41.933 --> 00:17:44.132
which is receptor-mediated endocytosis

00:17:44.133 --> 00:17:46.032
and after we had

00:17:46.033 --> 00:17:46.932
described this system

00:17:46.933 --> 00:17:48.432
for LDL, it turned out

00:17:48.433 --> 00:17:50.332
that other scientists

00:17:50.333 --> 00:17:51.932
found that other proteins

00:17:51.933 --> 00:17:53.299
bound to their own receptors

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:54.132
on the surface

00:17:54.133 --> 00:17:56.532
but they were taken in

00:17:56.533 --> 00:17:58.399
into the cell in the same

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:00.932
way as LDL.

00:18:00.933 --> 00:18:01.599
And the examples

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:03.399
include hormones such as insulin,

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:06.866
transport proteins such as transferrin,

00:18:06.867 --> 00:18:07.566
which transports

00:18:07.567 --> 00:18:09.866
iron and the protein

00:18:09.867 --> 00:18:11.566
that transports vitamin B12.

00:18:11.567 --> 00:18:14.166
So receptor mediated endocytosis

00:18:14.167 --> 00:18:15.266
turned out to be

00:18:15.267 --> 00:18:17.532
a general propery

00:18:17.533 --> 00:18:19.866
of all cells, at least all

00:18:19.867 --> 00:18:21.499
nucleated cells, and

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:23.399
it's interesting

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:24.432
that we had no idea

00:18:24.433 --> 00:18:25.632
that we would stumble

00:18:25.633 --> 00:18:26.566
into something that was so

00:18:26.567 --> 00:18:27.632
general, because all we

00:18:27.633 --> 00:18:28.499
wanted to do was

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:29.532
to figure out what was wrong

00:18:29.533 --> 00:18:32.066
with these children with FH.

00:18:32.067 --> 00:18:35.266
Brown: So, now this slide

00:18:35.267 --> 00:18:36.632
shows what we looked like

00:18:36.633 --> 00:18:37.599
when we started,

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:38.899
and what we look like now.

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:40.299
I think it is important

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:42.599
for any young people looking

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:44.732
at this video not to picture us

00:18:44.733 --> 00:18:46.232
the way are now

00:18:46.233 --> 00:18:47.566
as shown on the right.

00:18:47.567 --> 00:18:50.499
But picture us as we were in 1972,

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:53.632
when I was 31 years old

00:18:53.633 --> 00:18:55.066
and Joe was 32 years old.

00:18:55.067 --> 00:18:57.166
That is when we

00:18:57.167 --> 00:18:59.099
made these big discoveries

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:03.566
and the thrill of discovery

00:19:03.567 --> 00:19:04.166
when you are young

00:19:04.167 --> 00:19:05.766
and working hard

00:19:05.767 --> 00:19:08.099
and something happens like that

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:09.732
FH experiment

00:19:09.733 --> 00:19:10.632
that Joe described

00:19:10.633 --> 00:19:13.332
the thrill of that is amazing.

00:19:13.333 --> 00:19:14.932
And I want to say one other thing,

00:19:14.933 --> 00:19:17.632
that it is a fantastic thing

00:19:17.633 --> 00:19:18.632
to share that experience

00:19:18.633 --> 00:19:19.832
with somebody else.

00:19:19.833 --> 00:19:20.999
We have the longest

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:22.466
running partnership in

00:19:22.467 --> 00:19:24.866
we think in the history of science

00:19:24.867 --> 00:19:26.399
to be honest.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:30.566
And I just have a

00:19:30.567 --> 00:19:31.632
hard time understanding

00:19:31.633 --> 00:19:32.366
why somebody

00:19:32.367 --> 00:19:33.299
would want to do science

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:35.099
totally on their own without

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:36.999
somebody to share the adventure.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:39.199
You wouldn't want to take a trip to Paris

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:40.399
by yourself, would you?

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:42.366
I mean you want somebody along

00:19:42.367 --> 00:19:44.199
who can share the excitement

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:45.532
of that adventure

00:19:45.533 --> 00:19:47.799
and that's what Joe and I have had.

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:49.199
So it's been

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.232
it's a fantastic thing

00:19:51.233 --> 00:19:53.933
to have a partnership in science.

